Getting Started — aictrl.dev Documentation Documentation Menu Home / Docs / Getting Started Getting Started Install the public aictrl.dev skills and MCP integration, connect your GitHub repos, and build your first Knowledge Graph. Compare the supported agent connections on the Plugins and integrations overview. For enterprise adoption strategies, see our Enterprise Guide to AI Grounding . Before using generated findings, explanations, or workflow results, read our AI guidance, limitations, and feedback options . Just want a code review running on one repository? Use the activation hub and its visual guides for model access , GitHub setup , the first review , explaining PRs , taking a chat request to a PR , workflow configuration , starting workflows from GitHub , and recovery . Prerequisites Before you begin, make sure you have the following: A supported agent: Claude Code , Codex , OpenCode , Cursor , or Gemini CLI Node.js 22.20+ for package-based skill installation An aictrl account created with one of the sign-in methods currently offered in the app Tip Already using an AI coding agent? Add the public skills and the hosted aictrl.dev MCP server without changing your repository workflow. Installation Claude Code users can install the public plugin directly from the canonical aictrl-dev/skills repository: Claude Code Copy /plugin marketplace add aictrl-dev/skills
/plugin install aictrl@aictrl-public Then authenticate the plugin-provided MCP server from your terminal: Terminal Copy $ claude mcp login plugin:aictrl:aictrl One server URL Every supported client connects to https://aictrl.dev/mcp . OAuth identifies the user; tools use organization_id when scope is required, and the server verifies current membership. Info The MCP server uses HTTP transport. No local installation required — aictrl.dev runs as a hosted service. Your agent communicates with it over HTTPS. Quick Start Once installed, connect a repository and build your first Knowledge Graph to see aictrl.dev in action. Install the public integration — use the commands above for Claude Code, or follow the agent-specific setup matrix for Codex, OpenCode, Cursor, and Gemini CLI. Connect a GitHub repo — open Admin → Git Sync , install the GitHub App, choose its repository scope, then connect one of those repositories to aictrl. Follow the visual GitHub setup guide . Build a Knowledge Graph — once the repo is connected, trigger a Knowledge Graph build from the dashboard. aictrl.dev will analyze your codebase structure, extract entities and relationships, and create a queryable graph. Query your codebase — ask your AI agent questions about your code. The agent uses query_context to query the Knowledge Graph and returns structurally-aware answers, not just text search results. Track work with skills — use skills to define reusable workflows. Your agent calls update_backlog to log progress against epics and tasks in the aictrl.dev backlog. Info Each of these steps has a detailed guide. See the Platform Features section in the sidebar for full documentation on each capability. Platform Features Explore each feature in depth, in the recommended reading order. 1 Plugin Setup Install public skills, authenticate the single hosted MCP server with OAuth, and connect your AI agent to aictrl.dev. Learn more → 2 GitHub Integration Connect repositories via the GitHub App, receive webhook events, and track pull requests and branches. Learn more → 3 PR Explanations Understand a pull request before you review it: a plain-language explanation with diagrams you can click through to the code, shareable on GitHub. Learn more → 4 Knowledge Graph Build a structural map of your codebase and query it with query_context for structurally-aware answers. Learn more → 5 Skills Library Create, govern, and share reusable AI agent skills with scope hierarchies, approval workflows, and GitOps sync. Learn more → 6 Task Templates Define reusable AI task configurations with parameters, async execution, and automated GitHub Checks integration. Learn more → 7 Delivery Analytics Understand the sources, reporting windows, metric definitions, proxies, and interpretation boundaries behind delivery signals. View methodology → MCP Configuration Public clients use one stable server name and one stable URL. Plugin installs provide this configuration automatically; portable clients can add the equivalent entry manually. Canonical Remote Server MCP configuration Copy {
  "mcpServers": {
    "aictrl": {
      "url": "https://aictrl.dev/mcp"
    }
  }
} OAuth and organization scope OAuth identifies your aictrl.dev account. Use list_organizations to discover accessible IDs; tools that require scope accept organization_id and verify live membership. API keys are optional for local skill-usage telemetry and do not belong in this MCP configuration or URL. Workflow-as-Code Workflows are declarative YAML files that chain skills into a repeatable pipeline — implement-and-review loops, triage, releases, anything you'd otherwise script by hand. Author them in your repo, commit them, and aictrl applies them automatically. Add a trigger and they start on their own from GitHub events. The .aictrl/workflows/ convention aictrl scans one fixed path in each connected repo: .aictrl/workflows/<name>.yaml . Files must sit directly in that directory — nested subfolders are ignored — and the file's kebab-case <name> should match the workflow's own name field. Repo layout Copy .aictrl/
  workflows/
    hello-review.yaml
    release-pipeline.yaml Apply loop: commit → git-sync → live Workflow files apply through your normal GitOps flow: Commit your workflow file under .aictrl/workflows/ , typically as a pull request in your own repo so it goes through your usual review process. Merge the pull request to your repo's default branch. git-sync applies it — aictrl's webhook picks up the push, parses the file, and validates it against the workflow schema before making it live for your org. Fail-closed on unknown refs A node's skill , template or workflow reference must resolve to an existing, active one in your org by its portable name — never a raw ID. If a ref can't be resolved, or the file fails schema validation, the whole file is rejected. Nothing partial is ever applied. Minimal example A workflow needs a schemaVersion , a name , and at least one node. This one reviews a pull request as soon as it is marked ready, using the code-review skill: .aictrl/workflows/review-when-ready.yaml Copy schemaVer
